    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (78.09) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.90) lead our lineup, both benefiting immensely from plus matchups against starters with xERAs north of 5.30. **James Wood** is our premier play, capitalizing on his elite xwOBA and a favorable batting order position, while **Tyler Soderstrom** utilizes a strong park factor and a soft SP matchup to anchor our output. We are also leaning heavily on **Austin Riley** (73.32) and **Brice Turang** (71.09); **Austin Riley** gets a significant boost from a high-yield park, and **Brice Turang** remains a staple due to the high SP skill gap\u2014nearly a 20% efficiency surge\u2014against a vulnerable arm. **Corbin Carroll** (66.25) remains an essential start despite a tougher matchup against Griffin; his sprint speed and the 100% stolen base expectation against this pitcher's slow delivery make him an indispensable catalyst in our race to keep SB numbers competitive.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who missed the cut at 53.39 despite a solid profile. He was ultimately squeezed out by the high-floor production of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (63.00), who benefits from a more favorable platoon advantage today. While **Josh Naylor** provides reliable depth, the optimizer prioritized the specific platoon and SP-skill advantages inherent in **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2019s matchup against Mize. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Jose Caballero** and **Henry Bolte**; both draw lower scores due to bottom-of-the-order slots and specific catcher/pitcher defensive variables that limit their SB upside. **Gabriel Moreno** also sits near our marginal threshold (51.70), but his spot in the three-hole offers enough stability to keep him active. We are monitoring all lineups closely, as any late defensive shifts against our bottom-order hitters could necessitate a quick pivot to keep our game caps optimized for the final stretch of the week.",
